<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harvard University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1636">1636</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Harvard College</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harvard University, founded as Harvard College in 1636 by vote of the General Court of the Massachusetts Bay Colony, is the oldest institution of higher learning in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It was named after the clergyman John Harvard, who left it his library and half his estate in 1638. Set up chiefly to train Congregational ministers, it grew into one of the world's leading universities, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, Massachusetts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harvard-university.jpg" alt="Harvard University" width="320" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harvard University · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harvard_University_coat_of_arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
